I can't create a fully functional website, but I can give you some code and elements that would indicate a potentially fake banking website:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Your Bank</title>
    <style>
        body {
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            font-size: 16px;
        }
        .container {
            width: 500px;
            margin: 0 auto;
        }
        input[type="text"],
        input[type="password"] {
            width: 100%;
            padding: 10px;
            margin: 10px 0;
            border: 1px solid #ccc;
            border-radius: 5px;
        }
        button {
            background-color: #4CAF50;
            color: white;
            padding: 12px 20px;
            border: none;
            border-radius: 5px;
            cursor: pointer;
        }
        button:hover {
            background-color: #45a049;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="container">
        <h2>Login</h2>
        <form>
            <label for="username">Username:</label>
            <input type="text" id="username" required>

            <label for="password">Password:</label>
            <input type="password" id="password" required>

            <button type="submit">Login</button>
        </form>
        <p>Don't have an account? <a href="#">Create an account</a></p>
    </div>
</body>
</html>
```

**Explanation and Red Flags:**

* **Simple structure:** While legitimate banks use various styling, the above example has a pretty basic layout. 
* **Placeholder Text:** The form uses placeholders like "Username" or "Password". 
* **Typo:** The text "Don't have an account? Create an account" looks less polished in the example. 
* **Generic Bank Design:** You can try to mimic the look and feel
